Financial Toxicity of Disability / Chronic Illness - Adjustment Disorder - V60.2 (Extreme poverty) / V60.9 (Unspecified housing or economic problem)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Medical care rationing, Medical debt rumination, Benefit precariousness anxiety

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-017
Reason for Visit: Acute physical deterioration and severe exacerbation of underlying chronic illness secondary to medication rationing and extreme psychosocial stress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute exacerbation of the patient's primary physical illness symptoms, accompanied by severe lethargy and a 72-hour period of severe sleep deprivation due to financial panic, requiring immediate assessment to prevent catastrophic physiological collapse prior to routine GP hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I had to halve my pills to make them last until payday, and now my body is failing, but I can't afford an ambulance.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of severe financial toxicity related to their disability, characterized by pervasive dread regarding opening mail and relentless anxiety regarding the precariousness of disability support benefits. Over the past two weeks, due to insurmountable out-of-pocket costs, the patient engaged in dangerous medical care rationing, splitting essential medications and reducing food intake to stretch funds. This directly caused an acute exacerbation of their primary physical illness today. The patient describes chronic sleep deprivation caused by late-night medical debt rumination, profound feelings of shame and inadequacy, and strained interpersonal relationships due to the inability to meet financial obligations. They feel they are a severe financial burden on their extended family.
Functional Status: Acutely impaired; bedbound for the last 24 hours due to weakness and disease exacerbation.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe morbidity or mortality due to inability to afford essential life-sustaining medications and basic nutrition.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 108 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 62 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, cachectic, deeply exhausted, and tearful, exhibiting a flat affect and psychomotor slowing.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, weak peripheral pulses, delayed capillary refill (3 seconds). Respiratory: Clear to auscultation, shallow respiratory effort. Abdomen: Scaphoid, soft, non-tender. Neurological: Alert but cognitively sluggish. Cranial nerves intact. Motor strength is globally reduced (4/5) secondary to poor nutrition and medication withdrawal. Medical care rationing is evident; empty and half-cut pill bottles are scattered on the bedside table. The patient exhibits severe benefit precariousness anxiety, repeatedly asking the clinician not to bill them for the visit.
Environmental Observations: Refrigerator is nearly empty; a stack of unopened medical bills is visible on the kitchen counter. Heating is turned off despite the cold ambient temperature.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has intentionally halved the dose of their primary chronic illness medication for the last 14 days; currently completely out of medication.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Financial Toxicity of Disability / Chronic Illness - Adjustment Disorder - V60.2 (Extreme poverty) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, localized signs of infection, and clear correlation of the physical decline with the precise timeline of medication cessation and caloric restriction.

Primary Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the profound despair and sleep deprivation are directly and explicitly linked to objective, severe socioeconomic deprivation and debt, rather than endogenous melancholia.

Adrenal Insufficiency - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, though relative hypocortisolemia from chronic stress and poor nutrition is contributing to the mild hypotension; patient has no history of chronic steroid use.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute physiological decompensation, including hypotension (BP 95/60), reactive tachycardia (HR 108), and global weakness, directly resulting from the forced rationing of essential medications and food due to financial toxicity. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to assess the severity of the primary illness exacerbation, provide urgent bridge prescriptions to prevent further physical collapse, and assess for the need for emergency hospital admission. Delaying assessment until routine hours posed an imminent risk of severe, irreversible morbidity from medication withdrawal, strictly satisfying the necessity for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided an urgent, no-cost bridge prescription/sample of the primary chronic illness medication to restore therapeutic levels immediately. Advised the usual GP to formally document severe socioeconomic deprivation on social determinants of health screening and refer to hospital social work.
Positive Behavioral Change: To safely mitigate the immediate physical decline, implement a "hydration and simple calorie protocol": consume one glass of water and one small, available carbohydrate source every 2 hours while awake; logging this basic intake will positively reinforce physical stabilization and restore a minor sense of control. To address the medical debt rumination, designate a single, physical "mail bin" and agree to only open one piece of mail per week alongside a trusted support person; reducing the daily exposure to the stressor will improve sleep architecture.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community social work, financial counseling, and local food bank delivery services.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review within 24 hours to monitor physical recovery from medication resumption and to initiate urgent social support applications.

